    summary: "Helium is a colorless, odorless, tasteless, non-toxic, inert, monatomic gas, the first in the noble gas group in the periodic table.", 
    details: "Helium is the second lightest and second most abundant element in the observable universe, being present at about 24% of the total elemental mass, which is more than 12 times the mass of all the heavier elements combined.\n\n### Discovery\nHelium was first detected as an unknown yellow spectral line signature in sunlight during a solar eclipse in 1868 by Georges Rayet, C. T. Haig, Norman Pogson, and John Herschel. It was later independently discovered by French astronomer Pierre Janssen and English astronomer Norman Lockyer.\n\n### Physical Properties\nIn the gaseous state, helium consists of single atoms and is the least chemically reactive element. It has the lowest boiling and melting points among all the elements and it exists only as a gas except in extreme conditions. At extremely low temperatures, it becomes a superfluid, allowing it to flow with zero viscosity.\n\n### Applications\nHelium is used in cryogenics (its largest use, which consumes about a quarter of production), particularly in the cooling of superconducting magnets, with the main commercial application being in MRI scanners. Other industrial uses include as a pressurizing and purge gas, as a protective atmosphere for arc welding and in processes such as growing crystals to make silicon wafers.\n\n### Scarcity\nHelium is a non-renewable resource on Earth. Most of the helium in the atmosphere escapes into space. It is primarily produced by the alpha decay of heavy radioactive elements (thorium and uranium) in the Earth's crust, becoming trapped in natural gas deposits.",
