    category: "alkaline earth metal", 
    group: 2, 
    period: 2, 
    summary: "Beryllium is a steel-gray, strong, lightweight and brittle alkaline earth metal with the symbol Be and atomic number 4.", 
    details: "Beryllium is a relatively rare element in the universe, usually occurring as a product of the spallation of larger atomic nuclei that have collided with cosmic rays. In the Earth's crust, it is found in various minerals, most notably beryl (aquamarine, emerald) and bertrandite.\n\n### Physical Properties\nBeryllium has one of the highest melting points of the light metals. It has exceptional stiffness (Young's modulus 287 GPa) and thermal conductivity. Because of its low density and high thermal stability, it is an ideal material for aerospace and defense components.\n\n### Nuclear Applications\nBeryllium is used in nuclear reactors as a reflector or moderator because it has a low thermal neutron absorption cross-section. It is also used as a neutron source; when bombarded by alpha particles, it releases neutrons.\n\n### Aerospace & High-Tech\nDue to its stiffness, light weight and dimensional stability over a wide temperature range, beryllium metal is used for lightweight structural components in the defense and aerospace industries in high-speed aircraft, guided missiles, space vehicles, and satellites. It is a critical component of the James Webb Space Telescope's primary mirrors.\n\n### Health & Safety\nThe toxicity of beryllium is a major concern. Beryllium and its compounds are classified as Group 1 carcinogens. Inhalation of beryllium dust or fumes can lead to a serious chronic lung condition called berylliosis.",
