    summary: "Fluorine is the most electronegative element and is extremely reactive: almost all other elements, including some noble gases, form compounds with fluorine.", 
    details: "Fluorine is a chemical element with the symbol F and atomic number 9. It is the lightest halogen and exists as a highly toxic, pale yellow diatomic gas at standard conditions.\n\n### Extreme Reactivity\nAs the most electronegative element, fluorine is extremely reactive. It reacts with almost all organic and inorganic substances. Even items that are usually considered inert, like glass and சில noble gases (xenon, radon, and krypton), can react with fluorine under specific conditions. Because of this reactivity, fluorine is never found in its elemental state in nature.\n\n### Industrial Uses\nFluorine is used in the production of low-friction plastics such as PTFE (Teflon), and refrigerants like Freon. It is also essential in the processing of nuclear fuel, specifically for the enrichment of uranium through uranium hexafluoride gas.\n\n### Biological and Dental Role\nFluoride ions are essential for the health of teeth and bones. Sodium fluoride or other fluoride compounds are often added to drinking water and toothpaste to prevent dental cavities by strengthening the tooth enamel and making it more resistant to acid attack.\n\n### History\nThe first person to isolate fluorine was the French chemist Henri Moissan in 1886, a feat that earned him the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1906. Before him, many scientists suffered serious injuries or even death while attempting to isolate this dangerous element.",
