    details: "Neon is a chemical element with the symbol Ne and atomic number 10. It is a noble gas and is chemically inert. It is the second-lightest noble gas, after helium.\n\n### Discovery and Abundance\nNeon was discovered in 1898 by the British chemists Sir William Ramsay and Morris W. Travers. While neon is the fifth most abundant chemical element in the universe by mass, it is very rare on Earth due to its high volatility and lack of chemical reactivity.\n\n### Iconic Glow\nNeon is most famous for its use in sign advertising. When an electric current is passed through neon gas in a vacuum tube, it emits a brilliant reddish-orange light. While 'neon signs' are now a generic term, other gases are used to produce different colors.\n\n### Other Applications\nNeon is used in high-voltage indicators and switching gear, lightning arresters, diving equipment, and lasers. Liquid neon is an important cryogenic refrigerant, with over 40 times the refrigerating capacity per unit volume of liquid helium.\n\n### Isotopic Composition\nNaturally occurring neon is composed of three stable isotopes: 20Ne, 21Ne, and 22Ne. It is primarily extracted from liquid air by fractional distillation.",
