    summary: "Sodium is a soft, silvery-white, highly reactive metal. Sodium is an alkali metal, being in group 1 of the periodic table.", 
    details: "Sodium is a chemical element with the symbol Na and atomic number 11. It is highly reactive and for this reason, it is never found in its free form in nature. Instead, it must be prepared from compounds.\n\n### Physical Properties\nAt standard temperature and pressure, sodium is a soft metal that can be easily cut with a knife. It is a good conductor of electricity and heat. When freshly cut, it has a bright, silvery luster that rapidly tarnishes in air as it reacts with oxygen and moisture to form sodium oxide and sodium hydroxide.\n\n### Chemical Reactivity\nSodium reacts explosively with water, producing sodium hydroxide and flammable hydrogen gas. This reaction is highly exothermic and can ignite the hydrogen produced. Due to its high reactivity, sodium is usually stored in mineral oil or an inert gas atmosphere.\n\n### Biological Importance\nSodium is an essential element for all animals and some plants. In humans, sodium ions are the primary cation in the extracellular fluid and are vital for maintaining osmotic pressure, fluid balance, and the transmission of nerve impulses through the body's sodium-potassium pump.\n\n### Common Compounds\nThe most common compound of sodium is sodium chloride (table salt). It is also found in many other minerals like sodalite and rock salt. Sodium is the sixth most abundant element in the Earth's crust.",
