    details: "Magnesium is a chemical element with the symbol Mg and atomic number 12. It is an alkaline earth metal and the eighth-most-abundant element in the Earth's crust by mass.\n\n### Physical and Chemical Properties\nMagnesium is a fairly strong, silvery-white, light-weight metal (one third lighter than aluminum). It tarnishes slightly when exposed to air, although unlike the other alkaline earth metals, an impervious layer of oxide protects it from further corrosion. It is highly flammable, especially when in powder or thin-stripe forms, burning with a brilliant white light.\n\n### Biological Role\nMagnesium is the eleventh most abundant element by mass in the human body and is essential to all cells and some 300 enzymes. Magnesium ions interact with polyphosphate compounds such as ATP, DNA, and RNA. Hundreds of enzymes require magnesium ions to function.\n\n### Industrial Applications\nMagnesium is the third-most-commonly-used structural metal, following iron and aluminum. Its main applications are, in order: aluminum alloys, die-casting (alloyed with zinc), removing sulfur in the production of iron and steel, and the production of titanium in the Kroll process.\n\n### Discovery\nThe name magnesium originates from the Greek word for a district in Thessaly called Magnesia. It is related to magnetite and manganese, which also originated from this area, and required differentiation as separate substances. In 1755, Joseph Black recognized magnesium as an element.",
