    period: 3, 
    summary: "Aluminium is a silvery-white, soft, non-magnetic and ductile metal in the boron group. By mass, it is the most abundant metal in Earth's crust.", 
    details: "Aluminium is a chemical element with the symbol Al and atomic number 13. It is the third most abundant element in the Earth's crust after oxygen and silicon. Unlike most other metals, aluminium is rarely found in its pure form in nature; it is typically found in minerals like bauxite.\n\n### Physical Characteristics\nAluminium is remarkable for its low density and its ability to resist corrosion through the phenomenon of passivation. This is due to the formation of a thin, protective layer of aluminium oxide on the surface of the metal when it is exposed to air.\n\n### Industrial Applications\nAluminium and its alloys are vital to the aerospace industry and important in transportation and building industries, such as building facades and window frames. The most useful compounds of aluminium, at least on a weight basis, are the oxides and sulfates.\n\n### Recycling\nAluminium is 100% recyclable without any loss of its natural qualities. Recovery of the metal through recycling has become an important facet of the aluminium industry. Recycling aluminium requires only 5% of the energy needed to produce the primary metal from ore.\n\n### Discovery\nThe first person to isolate aluminium was the Danish physicist Hans Christian Ørsted in 1825. Industrial production of the metal began in the late 19th century with the development of the Hall-Héroult process.",
