    summary: "Silicon is a hard, brittle crystalline solid with a blue-grey metallic luster. It is a tetravalent metalloid and semiconductor.", 
    details: "Silicon is a chemical element with the symbol Si and atomic number 14. It is the eighth most abundant element in the universe by mass, but very rarely occurs as the pure element in the Earth's crust. It is most widely distributed in dusts, sands, planetoids, and planets as various forms of silicon dioxide (silica) or silicates.\n\n### The Silicon Age\nSilicon is the basis of modern electronics. Its semi-conducting properties are fundamental to the manufacture of transistors and integrated circuits used in computers, smartphones, and almost all electronic devices. Silicon Valley is named after this element due to its critical role in the technology industry.\n\n### Geological Importance\nOver 90% of the Earth's crust is composed of silicate minerals, making silicon the second most abundant element in the crust (about 28% by mass) after oxygen. Quartz is a common form of silicon dioxide.\n\n### Solar Energy\nSilicon is the primary material used in the production of photovoltaic cells, which convert sunlight into electricity. High-purity 'solar-grade' silicon is essential for the efficiency of solar panels.\n\n### Industrial Uses\nBeyond electronics, silicon is used in the production of steel, aluminum alloys, and silicones—polymers used in sealants, adhesives, lubricants, and medical applications.",
