    details: "Sulfur is a chemical element with the symbol S and atomic number 16. It is abundant, multivalent and nonmetallic. Under normal conditions, sulfur atoms form cyclic octatomic molecules with a chemical formula S8.\n\n### History and Occurence\nSulfur was known in ancient times and is referred to in the Bible as brimstone. It occurs naturally as the pure element (often near volcanoes) and as sulfide and sulfate minerals. Historically, it was a key component of gunpowder.\n\n### Industrial Importance\nToday, almost all elemental sulfur is produced as a byproduct of removing sulfur-containing contaminants from natural gas and petroleum. The greatest commercial use of the element is the production of sulfuric acid, which is the world's most produced chemical and is used in a vast array of industrial processes, particularly fertilizer manufacturing.\n\n### Biological Role\nSulfur is an essential element for all life. It is a constituent of several amino acids (cysteine and methionine) and therefore of many proteins. It is also a part of vitamin B1 (thiamine) and biotin. Disulfide bonds between cysteine residues contribute significantly to the 3D structure and strength of proteins, such as keratin in hair and nails.\n\n### Physical Properties\nSulfur is a poor conductor of heat and electricity. It is insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulfide. When burned, sulfur melts to a blood-red liquid and emits a blue flame, producing sulfur dioxide gas which has a suffocating odor.",
