    summary: "Potassium is a soft silvery-white alkali metal that oxidizes rapidly in air and reacts vigorously with water.", 
    details: "Potassium is a chemical element with the symbol K and atomic number 19. It was first isolated from potash, the ashes of plants, from which its name derives. In the periodic table, potassium is one of the alkali metals.\n\n### Reactivity\nLike all alkali metals, potassium has a single valence electron in its outer shell, which it easily loses to form a positive ion. This makes it extremely reactive. Elemental potassium reacts violently with water, generating enough heat to ignite the hydrogen gas produced and burn with a lilac-colored flame. It must be stored under mineral oil or in an inert atmosphere.\n\n### Biological Importance\nPotassium is an essential mineral for all living organisms. In animals, it is the major intracellular cation. It is vital for maintaining nerve function, muscle contraction (including the heart), and fluid balance. A delicate balance of potassium and sodium ions across cell membranes is maintained by the sodium-potassium pump.\n\n### Agricultural Uses\nMost of the potassium produced worldwide is used in agriculture as fertilizer (often in the form of potassium chloride or potassium sulfate). It is one of the three primary nutrients required by plants, along with nitrogen and phosphorus (the 'N-P-K' on fertilizer bags).\n\n### Common Sources\nPotassium does not occur as a free element in nature due to its high reactivity. It is found in many minerals, such as sylvite and carnallite, often formed by the evaporation of ancient seas.",
