    category: "transition metal", 
    group: 7, 
    period: 4, 
    summary: "Manganese is a silver-grey metal that resembles iron. It is hard and very brittle, difficult to fuse, but easy to oxidize.", 
    details: "Manganese is a chemical element with the symbol Mn and atomic number 25. It is found as a free element in nature (often in combination with iron), and in many minerals. Manganese is a transition metal with a multifaceted range of industrial alloy uses, particularly in stainless steels.\n\n### Steel Production\nHistorically, manganese is named for its similarity to magnetite, which was found in the same region as pyrolusite (manganese dioxide). Manganese is essential to iron and steel production by virtue of its sulfur-fixing, deoxidizing, and alloying properties. It improves the strength, toughness, and wear resistance of steel.\n\n### Biological Role\nManganese is an essential trace nutrient in all known forms of life. It acts as a cofactor for many enzymes, including those involved in antioxidant protection and the metabolism of carbohydrates and proteins. In plants, manganese is critical for the oxygen-evolving complex in photosynthesis.\n\n### Everyday Applications\nManganese dioxide is used as an oxidizer in alkaline and dry-cell batteries. It is also used as a pigment and as a catalyst in various chemical reactions.\n\n### Scarcity and Sources\nMost manganese is extracted from manganese ores, primarily in South Africa, Australia, China, and Gabon. It is the 12th most abundant element in the Earth's crust.",
