    name: "Copper", 
    atomicMass: "63.546", 
    category: "transition metal", 
    group: 11, 
    period: 4, 
    summary: "Copper is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color.", 
    details: "Copper is a chemical element with the symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is one of the few metals that can occur in nature in a directly usable metallic form (native metals). This led to very early human use in several regions, from c. 8000 BC.\n\n### Historical Significance\nCopper was the first metal to be smelted from its ore (c. 5000 BC), the first metal to be cast into a shape in a mold (c. 4000 BC), and the first metal to be purposefully alloyed with another metal, tin, to create bronze (c. 3500 BC).\n\n### Electrical and Thermal Conductivity\nCopper is widely used as a conductor of heat and electricity. It is the standard against which other conductors are measured. Its high conductivity and ductility make it ideal for electrical wiring, motors, and electronic components.\n\n### Architectural and Decorative Uses\nCopper's distinctive color and its ability to develop a protective green patina (verdigris) when exposed to the elements make it a popular material for roofing, guttering, and architectural cladding. It is also used in jewelry and decorative art.\n\n### Bactericidal Properties\nCopper and its alloys (such as brass and bronze) are antimicrobial. They can kill a wide range of bacteria and viruses on contact, making copper surfaces useful in healthcare environments to prevent the spread of infections.",
