    category: "post-transition metal", 
    group: 13, 
    period: 4, 
    summary: "Gallium is a soft, silvery-blue metal at standard temperature and pressure. It has an unusually low melting point, melting in the hand.", 
    details: "Gallium is a chemical element with the symbol Ga and atomic number 31. It does not occur as a free element in nature, but as gallium(III) compounds in trace amounts in zinc ores and in bauxite.\n\n### Low Melting Point\nThe most striking property of gallium is its very low melting point (29.7°C or 85.5°F), which is just above room temperature. This means it can melt if held in a warm hand. However, it stays in the liquid phase over a very wide temperature range (boiling point 2229°C), giving it the largest liquid range of any element.\n\n### High-Tech Electronics\nMost gallium produced is used in electronics. The primary compound, gallium arsenide (GaAs), is used in microwave circuits, high-speed switching circuits, and infrared-emitting diodes (LEDs). Gallium nitride (GaN) is essential for blue and violet LEDs and high-power high-frequency electronics.\n\n### Scientific and Medical Uses\nGallium is used as a non-toxic substitute for mercury in high-temperature thermometers. It is also used in some specialized medical scans (gallium scans) to detect inflammation, infection, or certain types of tumors.\n\n### Fun Fact\nDue to its low melting point and the fact that it expands as it solidifies (like water), liquid gallium is sometimes used in 'magic tricks' and science demonstrations to create vanishing spoons or to break glass containers upon freezing.",
