    category: "metalloid", 
    group: 14, 
    period: 4, 
    summary: "Germanium is a lustrous, hard-brittle, greyish-white metalloid in the carbon group. It is chemically similar to its group neighbors silicon and tin.", 
    details: "Germanium is a chemical element with the symbol Ge and atomic number 32. It was discovered in 1886 by Clemens Winkler, who named it after his home country, Germany (Germania).\n\n### Fiber Optics and Infrared Optics\nThe single largest use of germanium is in fiber-optic systems, where it is used to increase the refractive index of glass fibers. It is also transparent to infrared light, making it a critical material for high-performance infrared optical systems, such as night-vision systems and thermal imaging cameras.\n\n### Semiconductor History\nGermanium played a key role in the early history of the semiconductor industry. The first transistor, developed at Bell Labs in 1947, was made of germanium. While it has been largely replaced by silicon for mainstream electronics, it is still used in high-efficiency solar cells and specialized high-speed transistors.\n\n### Specialized Catalysts\nGermanium dioxide is used as a catalyst in the production of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) plastic. This plastic is used for beverage bottles and food containers. About 25% of germanium production is used in this industry.\n\n### Characteristics\nGermanium is a semiconductor that is fairly brittle. Like water and gallium, it expands as it freezes from the liquid state. Pure germanium is a semiconductor with an appearance very similar to elemental silicon.",
