    category: "metalloid", 
    group: 15, 
    period: 4, 
    summary: "Arsenic is a metalloid that occurs in many minerals, usually in combination with sulfur and metals. It is infamous for its toxicity.", 
    details: "Arsenic is a chemical element with the symbol As and atomic number 33. It is a well-known metalloid that has been recognized since ancient times. It occurs in three allotropic forms: grey, yellow, and black.\n\n### Toxicity and Health\nArsenic is most infamous as a potent poison and carcinogen. Long-term exposure to arsenic through drinking water can lead to chronic arsenic poisoning, causing skin lesions, developmental issues, and various types of cancer. Despite this, some arsenic compounds have been used in medicine, such as the treatment of certain types of leukemia.\n\n### Industrial and Agricultural History\nHistorically, arsenic was used in pesticides, herbicides, and as a wood preservative (chromated copper arsenate). Due to its high toxicity, these uses have been heavily restricted or banned in many countries. It was also used as a pigment (Paris Green) in the 19th century.\n\n### Semiconductor Industry\nIn modern times, the most important use of arsenic is as a doping agent in the semiconductor industry. It is used to create N-type semiconductor layers in silicon devices. Gallium arsenide (GaAs) is a critical component in high-speed electronics and LEDs.\n\n### Metallurgy\nSmall amounts of arsenic are added to lead alloys (such as in lead-acid car batteries) to improve their strength and durability. It is also used in some bronze and copper alloys.",
