    period: 4, 
    summary: "Selenium is a nonmetal with properties that are intermediate between the elements above and below in the periodic table, sulfur and tellurium.", 
    details: "Selenium is a chemical element with the symbol Se and atomic number 34. It is found in trace amounts in many minerals but is rarely found as the pure element in nature.\n\n### Photoelectric Properties\nThe most important property of selenium is its photoconductivity—its electrical conductivity increases when exposed to light. This led to its early use in light sensors, exposure meters for cameras, and the first solar cells. It is also a semiconductor, making it useful in rectifiers for converting AC to DC electricity.\n\n### Glass and Ceramics\nThe majority of selenium production is used in the glass industry. It is used to decolorize glass or to impart a deep red color to glass and ceramics. It is also used in the manufacture of enamels and specialized pigments.\n\n### Biological Importance\nSelenium is an essential trace nutrient for many organisms, including humans. it is a constituent of several important enzymes (selenoproteins), notably glutathione peroxidase, which helps protect cells from oxidative damage. However, it is only required in tiny amounts; too much selenium can be toxic (selenosis).\n\n### Dietary Sources\nBrazil nuts are the richest dietary source of selenium. Other sources include seafood, meat, grain, and dairy products. The amount of selenium in plant foods depends on the selenium content of the soil where the plants were grown.",
