    summary: "Bromine is a reddish-brown liquid at room temperature that evaporates readily to form a similarly colored vapor.", 
    details: "Bromine is a chemical element with the symbol Br and atomic number 35. It is the third-lightest halogen and is a volatile red-brown liquid at room temperature. Its name is derived from the Greek word 'bromos', meaning 'stench', due to its sharp and unpleasant smell.\n\n### Physical Properties\nBromine is the only nonmetallic element that is liquid at standard conditions. It is highly reactive and corrosive. It dissolves in water but is very soluble in organic solvents. Like other halogens, it is never found as the pure element in nature but only in halide salts.\n\n### Industrial Applications\nThe largest use of bromine is in the production of brominated flame retardants. These are chemicals added to plastics, textiles, and electronics to slow or prevent the spread of fire. Other uses include pharmaceuticals, photography (silver bromide), and as a disinfectant for water treatment.\n\n### Toxicity and Safety\nElemental bromine is toxic and can cause severe burns to the skin and mucous membranes. Inhalation of bromine vapor is highly irritating and can be fatal at high concentrations. Because of its toxicity, it must be handled with extreme care and stored in specialized containers.\n\n### Extraction\nBromine is primarily extracted from bromide-rich brine deposits found in various parts of the world, most notably in the Dead Sea and from underground brine sources in the United States and China.",
