    period: 4, 
    summary: "Krypton is a colorless, odorless, tasteless noble gas that occurs in trace amounts in the atmosphere.", 
    details: "Krypton is a chemical element with the symbol Kr and atomic number 36. It is a member of the noble gas group. It is about three times heavier than air and is one of the rarest gases in Earth's atmosphere, present at only about 1 part per million.\n\n### Lighting Applications\nKrypton is used in several types of lighting. It is often used in energy-efficient fluorescent lamps and in high-intensity discharge lamps for large-area lighting. Krypton gas is also used in high-speed photography flashes and in airport approach lights due to its brilliant white glow when ionized.\n\n### Laser Technology\nKrypton fluoride (KrF) is an important medium in excimer lasers. These lasers produce powerful pulses of ultraviolet light and are used in deep ultraviolet lithography for the manufacture of integrated circuits, as well as in some medical and scientific research applications.\n\n### Scientific Definition of the Meter\nFrom 1960 to 1983, the international standard for the meter was defined based on the wavelength of light emitted from a specific transition in krypton-86 atoms. This replaced the previous physical prototype meter bar and provided a more stable and high-precision reference.\n\n### Discovery\nKrypton was discovered in 1898 by the British chemists Sir William Ramsay and Morris W. Travers. They discovered it in the residue remaining from liquid air that had been boiled almost entirely away, shortly after discovering neon and just before discovering xenon.",
