    period: 5, 
    summary: "Rubidium is a soft, silvery-white metallic element of the alkali metal group. It is one of the most electropositive and alkaline elements.", 
    details: "Rubidium is a chemical element with the symbol Rb and atomic number 37. Like other alkali metals, it is highly reactive, with properties similar to those of other alkali metals, such as rapid oxidation in air.\n\n### Physical Characteristics\nRubidium is a soft, ductile, silvery-white metal. It is the second most electropositive of the stable alkali metals and melts at a temperature of 39.3 °C (102.7 °F). Like other alkali metals, it reacts violently with water and can ignite spontaneously in air.\n\n### Industrial and Scientific Uses\nRubidium and its compounds have few commercial uses. The metal is used in the manufacture of photocells and in the removal of residual gases from vacuum tubes. Rubidium salts are used in glasses and ceramics and in fireworks to give them a purple color. It has also been used in laser cooling and as a working fluid in vapor turbines.\n\n### Atomic Clocks\nOne of the most precise uses of rubidium is in rubidium atomic clocks. These clocks are used in global positioning systems (GPS) and other high-precision timing applications where stability and accuracy are critical.\n\n### Biological Role\nRubidium has no known biological role but may have a slight stimulatory effect on metabolism, possibly because it is similar to potassium. The human body tends to treat Rb+ ions as if they were potassium ions, and therefore concentrates rubidium in the body's intracellular fluid.",
