    category: "post-transition metal", 
    group: 14, 
    period: 5, 
    summary: "Tin is a silvery-white, malleable post-transition metal. It is well known for its resistance to corrosion and its use in alloys.", 
    details: "Tin is a chemical element with the symbol Sn and atomic number 50. It has been known and used by humans since ancient times, particularly in the production of bronze. It is obtained primarily from the mineral cassiterite.\n\n### Historical and Alloy Use\nTin was a critical component of the Bronze Age. When alloyed with copper, it results in bronze, which is much harder and more durable than pure copper. Another important tin alloy is pewter, which was traditionally used for tableware and ornaments.\n\n### Corrosion Protection and Tin Cans\nDue to its high resistance to corrosion and non-toxic nature, tin is used to coat other metals, primarily steel, to prevent trust and preserve food. This led to the development of 'tin cans'. Modern cans are actually steel coated with an extremely thin layer of tin.\n\n### Soldering and Electronics\nThe most significant modern use of tin is in electrical solder. Solder is an alloy of tin and lead (or increasingly just tin) used to create permanent connections between electronic components. Over half of all tin produced is used for this purpose.\n\n### Physical Properties\nTin is a silvery-white, soft metal that can be easily melted (melting point 231.9 °C). It exists in two main allotropic forms: white tin (metallic) and grey tin (nonmetallic powder). The transition from white to grey tin at low temperatures is known as 'tin pest,' which can cause tin objects to disintegrate in extreme cold.",
