    symbol: "Cs", 
    name: "Caesium", 
    atomicMass: "132.91", 
    category: "alkali metal", 
    group: 1, 
    period: 6, 
    summary: "Caesium is a soft, silvery-gold alkali metal. It is one of only five elemental metals that are liquid at or near room temperature.", 
    details: "Caesium is a chemical element with the symbol Cs and atomic number 55. It is a soft, silvery-gold alkali metal with a melting point of 28.5 °C (83.3 °F), which makes it one of only five elemental metals that are liquid at or near room temperature.\n\n### Atomic Clocks and Timekeeping\nThe most well-known use of caesium is in the caesium atomic clock. Since 1967, the International System of Units (SI) has defined the second as the duration of 9,192,631,770 cycles of the radiation corresponding to the transition between two hyperfine levels of the ground state of the caesium-133 atom. These clocks are incredibly accurate, losing only one second every few hundred million years.\n\n### Drilling and Industrial Uses\nCaesium formate is widely used as a high-density, low-viscosity fluid in the oil and gas industry for deep-hole drilling. Caesium compounds are also used in various specialized glasses, ceramics, and as a catalyst in organic chemistry. Caesium-137, a radioactive isotope, is used in medical radiation therapy and in industrial gauges for measuring fluid flow and density.\n\n### Extreme Reactivity\nCaesium is the most reactive of the stable alkali metals. It reacts explosively with water even at low temperatures and can ignite spontaneously in air. Due to this extreme reactivity, it must be handled with extreme care and stored in vacuum-sealed glass ampoules or under an inert gas.\n\n### Discovery\nCaesium was discovered in 1860 by Robert Bunsen and Gustav Kirchhoff using the then-newly developed method of flame spectroscopy. Its name is derived from the Latin word 'caesius,' meaning 'sky blue,' referring to the characteristic blue lines in its emission spectrum.",
    shells: [2, 8, 18, 18, 8, 1], 
    phase: "Solid" 
