    category: "lanthanide", 
    group: 7, 
    period: 9, 
    summary: "Promethium is a radioactive rare-earth element. All of its isotopes are radioactive and it is extremely rare in nature.", 
    details: "Promethium is a chemical element with the symbol Pm and atomic number 61. It is a member of the lanthanide series. All of its isotopes are radioactive, and it is the second-lightest element whose isotopes are all radioactive (after technetium).\n\n### Discovery and Occurrence\nPromethium was first produced synthetically at the Oak Ridge National Laboratory in 1945 by the fission of uranium. It doesn't occur in the Earth's crust in significant amounts; trace amounts have been found in uranium ores as a product of spontaneous fission. It has been detected in the spectra of some stars.\n\n### Luminous and Nuclear Uses\nDue to its radioactivity, promethium is used in specialized luminous paints for watch dials and aircraft instruments. It is also used as a beta radiation source for thickness gauges and in nuclear batteries, where the beta decay of promethium-147 is converted into electricity. These batteries are used in spacecraft and specialized medical devices.\n\n### Challenges and Safety\nBecause all of its isotopes are radioactive, promethium must be handled with extreme care in specialized laboratories. Its short half-life (Promethium-145 has a half-life of 17.7 years) and rarity make it difficult and expensive to produce and study.\n\n### Etymology\nThe element is named after Prometheus, the Titan from Greek mythology who stole fire from Mount Olympus and gave it to humanity, symbolizing the 'fire' of human ingenuity and the power of nuclear science.",
