    symbol: "Sm", 
    name: "Samarium", 
    atomicMass: "150.36", 
    category: "lanthanide", 
    group: 8, 
    period: 9, 
    summary: "Samarium is a moderately hard, silvery metal that slowly oxidizes in air. It is a rare-earth element and a member of the lanthanide series.", 
    details: "Samarium is a chemical element with the symbol Sm and atomic number 62. It is a moderately hard, silvery metal that slowly oxidizes in air. Although it's classified as a rare-earth element, it is actually more abundant in the Earth's crust than tin.\n\n### High-Temperature Magnets\nSamarium is most famous for its use in samarium-cobalt (SmCo) magnets. While not as strong as neodymium magnets at room temperature, SmCo magnets are much more resistant to heat and corrosion. They can operate at temperatures up to 700°C without losing their magnetic properties, making them critical for high-performance aerospace and industrial applications.\n\n### Cancer Treatment and Medicine\nRadioactive samarium-153 is used in medicine, particularly in the drug Quadramet, to treat bone pain associated with advanced cancers. It works by targeting the bone lesions and delivering a localized dose of beta radiation. Samarium is also used in some types of specialized medical imaging.\n\n### Industrial and Optical Uses\nSamarium is used as a dopant in high-quality optical glass to absorb infrared light. It is also used as a catalyst in various organic chemical reactions, such as the dehydration and dehydrogenation of alcohols. Samarium oxide is used in specialized ceramics and as a neutron absorber in nuclear reactor control rods.\n\n### Discovery\nSamarium was discovered in 1879 by the French chemist Lecoq de Boisbaudran. He identified it through its characteristic absorption lines in the mineral samarskite, which was named after a Russian mine official, Vasili Samarsky-Bykhovets.",
