    category: "lanthanide", 
    group: 9, 
    period: 9, 
    summary: "Europium is a moderately hard, silvery metal that is the most reactive of the rare-earth elements. It is primarily used for its phosphorescent properties.", 
    details: "Europium is a chemical element with the symbol Eu and atomic number 63. It is a member of the lanthanide series. Europium is the most reactive of the rare-earth elements, rapidly oxidizing in air and reacting with water similarly to calcium.\n\n### Displays and Phosphors\nThe most important use of europium is in the production of phosphors for various types of displays. Europium-doped compounds are used to produce the vibrant red and blue colors in CRT televisions, computer monitors, and LEDs. Without europium, early color televisions would have had very dull red colors.\n\n### Counterfeit Protection\nDue to its unique fluorescent properties, europium is used as a security feature in paper money. For example, some euro banknotes use europium-based inks that glow under ultraviolet light, making them much harder to counterfeit.\n\n### Nuclear and Industrial Uses\nEuropium is an excellent absorber of neutrons, making it a valuable material for control rods in nuclear reactors. It is also used as a dopant in high-quality optical glass and in specialized scientific research. Some europium compounds are used as biological probes to label and track molecules in research.\n\n### Discovery\nEuropium was discovered in 1896 by the French chemist Eugène-Anatole Demarçay. He suspected that samples of samarium were contaminated with an unknown element and eventually succeeded in isolating it in 1901. It was named after the continent of Europe.",
