    summary: "Dysprosium is a soft, silvery-white metallic element of the lanthanide group. It is best known for its use in permanent magnets.", 
    details: "Dysprosium is a chemical element with the symbol Dy and atomic number 66. It is a member of the lanthanide series and is a rare-earth element. It is relatively stable in air at room temperature but oxidizes when heated.\n\n### Enhancing Permanent Magnets\nThe most critical modern use of dysprosium is in the production of high-performance neodymium-iron-boron (NdFeB) magnets. Small amounts of dysprosium are added to these magnets to significantly improve their resistance to demagnetization, particularly at high temperatures. This makes them essential for electric vehicle motors and wind turbine generators.\n\n### Nuclear Industry\nDysprosium has a high cross-section for absorbing thermal neutrons, making it a valuable material for control rods in nuclear reactors. It can absorb neutrons without swelling or contracting, which provides stability in the extreme environment of a reactor core.\n\n### Lighting and Data Storage\nDysprosium is used in specialized high-intensity discharge lamps to produce a bright white light with a high color rendering index. It is also used in the manufacture of magneto-optical data storage discs and in specialized optical glass.\n\n### Discovery\nDysprosium was discovered in 1886 by the French chemist Lecoq de Boisbaudran. He identified it through its characteristic absorption spectrum in a sample of erbium oxide. Its name is derived from the Greek word 'dysprositos,' meaning 'difficult to get,' referring to the difficulty of isolating it from other rare-earth elements.",
