    category: "lanthanide", 
    group: 14, 
    period: 9, 
    summary: "Erbium is a silvery-white, malleable and ductile rare-earth metal. It is primarily used in fiber-optic communications and lasers.", 
    details: "Erbium is a chemical element with the symbol Er and atomic number 68. It is a member of the lanthanide series. It is relatively stable in air and does not oxidize as rapidly as some other rare-earth metals.\n\n### Fiber-Optic Amplifiers\nThe most critical modern use of erbium is in erbium-doped fiber amplifiers (EDFAs). These are used to amplify optical signals in long-distance fiber-optic cables. This technology is the backbone of the global internet, allowing signals to travel thousands of kilometers without needing expensive electronic regenerators.\n\n### Medical and Dental Lasers\nErbium-doped lasers (Er:YAG) emit light at a wavelength that is strongly absorbed by water and hydroxyapatite (the main component of tooth enamel and bone). This allows for highly precise and minimally invasive medical and dental procedures, such as 'painless' laser dentistry and specialized skin resurfacing treatments.\n\n### Glass and Ceramics\nErbium oxide is used to impart a beautiful pink color to glass and ceramics. It is also used in high-quality sunglasses and camera filters to absorb infrared light. Erbium is sometimes added to other alloys to improve their workability and resistance to corrosion.\n\n### Nuclear Industry\nErbium is used in nuclear technology as a 'burnable poison' in nuclear fuel. It can absorb neutrons efficiently, which helps to maintain a steady and safe rate of fission over the lifetime of the fuel assembly.\n\n### Discovery\nErbium was discovered in 1843 by the Swedish chemist Carl Gustaf Mosander. Like yttrium, terbium, and ytterbium, it was named after the Ytterby mine in Sweden where it was first identified.",
