    summary: "Thulium is the second-least abundant of the lanthanides. It is a silvery-grey, soft metal that can be cut with a knife.", 
    details: "Thulium is a chemical element with the symbol Tm and atomic number 69. It is the second-least abundant of the lanthanides, being even rarer than iodine in the Earth's crust. It is a soft, malleable, silvery-grey metal.\n\n### Portable X-ray Sources\nThulium is used to create portable X-ray devices. When thulium-169 is bombarded with neutrons in a nuclear reactor, it produces thulium-170, which emits X-rays. These small, relatively safe radioactive sources are used in portable medical and dental X-ray machines and in specialized industrial testing.\n\n### Laser Technology\nThulium-doped lasers are used in various medical and industrial applications. They emit light at a wavelength that is highly absorbed by tissue, making them useful for specialized surgical procedures. They are also being researched for use in high-performance LIDAR and atmospheric sensing systems.\n\n### Anti-Counterfeiting and Phosphors\nThulium is used in specialized phosphors that glow blue under ultraviolet light. This property is used as a security feature in some paper currencies and in the manufacture of high-efficiency fluorescent lamps. It is also used as a dopant in high-quality optical glass.\n\n### Discovery\nThulium was discovered in 1879 by the Swedish chemist Per Teodor Cleve. He identified it through its characteristic absorption spectrum while investigating samples of erbium oxide. It was named after 'Thule,' an ancient Greek and Roman name for a mythical region in the far north, possibly Scandinavia.",
