    summary: "Ytterbium is a soft, silvery-white rare-earth metal. It is one of the more common lanthanides and is used in specialized alloys and lasers.", 
    details: "Ytterbium is a chemical element with the symbol Yb and atomic number 70. It is the penultimate member of the lanthanide series. It is a soft, ductile metal that tarnishes in air and reacts slowly with water.\n\n### Fiber Lasers and Amplifiers\nYtterbium is a vital dopant in high-power fiber lasers. Ytterbium-doped lasers (Yb:YAG and Yb-doped fibers) are highly efficient and can produce incredibly powerful and precise beams. They are used in industrial laser cutting, welding, and in specialized scientific and medical research.\n\n### Atomic Clocks\nYtterbium is being used in the development of the next generation of atomic clocks. Optical lattice clocks based on ytterbium atoms are even more accurate than standard caesium atomic clocks, potentially providing a more precise way to define the second in the future.\n\n### Metallurgy and Scientific Sensors\nYtterbium is used as an alloying agent to improve the strength and mechanical properties of stainless steel and other metals. It is also used as a pressure sensor because its electrical resistance increases significantly when subjected to high pressure. Ytterbium compounds are also used in specialized catalysts.\n\n### Discovery\nYtterbium was discovered in 1878 by the Swiss chemist Jean Charles Galissard de Marignac. Like yttrium, terbium, and erbium, it was named after the Ytterby mine in Sweden. It was the last of the four elements named after the mine to be discovered.",
