    period: 9, 
    summary: "Lutetium is a silvery-white rare-earth metal that is the hardest and densest of the lanthanide series.", 
    details: "Lutetium is a chemical element with the symbol Lu and atomic number 71. It is the final element of the lanthanide series and is often considered the hardest and densest member of the group. It is a silvery-white, corrosion-resistant metal.\n\n### Cancer Therapy and Imaging\nRadioactive lutetium-177 is used in a groundbreaking form of targeted cancer therapy called peptide receptor radionuclide therapy (PRRT). Lutetium-177 is bound to molecules that target specific receptors on cancer cells, delivering a precise dose of radiation to kill the tumor while minimizing damage to healthy tissue. Lutetium is also used in PET scan detectors.\n\n### Industrial Catalysis and Research\nLutetium is used as a highly efficient catalyst in various industrial processes, such as the cracking of petroleum and in the polymerization of organic compounds. Due to its high density and hardness, it is also used in specialized research and as a dopant in some high-quality optical materials.\n\n### Scientific Dating\nThe lutetium-hafnium dating method is an important tool in geology and cosmochemistry. By measuring the ratio of lutetium-176 to its decay product hafnium-176, scientists can determine the age of ancient rocks, minerals, and meteorites with high precision.\n\n### Discovery\nLutetium was discovered independently in 1907 by the French scientist Georges Urbain, the Austrian mineralogist Baron Carl Auer von Welsbach, and the American chemist Charles James. Urbain named it after 'Lutetia,' the Latin name for Paris.",
    shells: [2, 8, 18, 32, 9, 2], 
    phase: "Solid" 
