    name: "Thallium", 
    atomicMass: "204.38", 
    category: "post-transition metal", 
    group: 13, 
    period: 6, 
    summary: "Thallium is a soft grey post-transition metal that is highly toxic. It tarnishes rapidly in air and looks like lead.", 
    details: "Thallium is a chemical element with the symbol Tl and atomic number 81. It is a soft, malleable grey metal that is so soft it can be cut with a knife. It tarnishes so rapidly that it is usually stored under oil to prevent oxidation.\n\n### Extreme Toxicity\nThallium and its compounds are extremely toxic and were formerly used as rat poison and insecticides. It is often referred to as 'the poisoner's poison' because it is odorless, tasteless, and its symptoms can mimic those of common illnesses. Its use in residential pesticides is now strictly banned in most countries.\n\n### Electronics and Glass\nThallium is used in the electronics industry for specialized semiconductor devices and in the production of high-refractive-index glass for lenses and prisms. Thallium sulfide's electrical conductivity changes significantly with exposure to infrared light, making it useful in some types of specialized sensors.\n\n### Medical Imaging\nRadioactive thallium-201 is used in nuclear medicine for cardiac stress tests (thallium scans) to evaluate blood flow to the heart muscle. It acts as a potassium analog and is taken up by healthy heart tissue, allowing doctors to identify areas of poor circulation or damage.\n\n### Discovery\nThallium was discovered in 1861 by William Crookes and independently by Claude-Auguste Lamy using the then-new method of flame spectroscopy. Its name is derived from the Greek 'thallos,' meaning a green shoot or twig, referring to the bright green line in its emission spectrum.",
