    period: 6, 
    summary: "Polonium is a rare and highly radioactive metal with no stable isotopes. It is chemically similar to selenium and tellurium.", 
    details: "Polonium is a chemical element with the symbol Po and atomic number 84. It is an extremely rare and volatile radioactive element. It occurs naturally in uranium ores as a product of radioactive decay.\n\n### Extreme Radioactivity and Heat\nPolonium is one of the most radioactive elements known. A single gram of polonium-210 can heat itself to over 500 °C through its own alpha decay. This property and its intense radiation make it extremely dangerous to handle. It has been used as a heat source for specialized satellite and space probe power systems (RTGs).\n\n### Industrial and Specialized Uses\nPolonium is used in some specialized industrial equipment to eliminate static electricity, such as in brushes for cleaning photographic film and in specialized laboratory equipment. However, because of its extreme toxicity and short half-life, it is being replaced by safer alternatives.\n\n### Toxicity and Safety\nPolonium-210 is one of the most lethal substances known to humanity if ingested or inhaled. It is millions of times more toxic than hydrogen cyanide. It emits intense alpha radiation that can cause massive organ damage. It gained global notoriety after its use in high-profile poisoning cases.\n\n### Discovery\nPolonium was discovered in 1898 by Marie and Pierre Curie while they were investigating the radioactivity of pitchblende. It was the first element Marie Curie discovered. She named it after her native land, Poland, to highlight its lack of independence at the time.",
