    period: 6, 
    summary: "Astatine is a very rare and highly radioactive element. It is the heaviest naturally occurring halogen.", 
    details: "Astatine is a chemical element with the symbol At and atomic number 85. It is the rarest naturally occurring element in the Earth's crust, with less than 30 grams estimated to exist on the entire planet at any given time because its isotopes decay so rapidly.\n\n### Radioactivity and Rarity\nAll isotopes of astatine are highly radioactive. The most stable isotope, astatine-210, has a half-life of only 8.1 hours. Because it is so rare and short-lived, it is extremely difficult to study. Most of what is known about it comes from its position in the periodic table and from studying tiny, synthetically produced samples.\n\n### Potential Medical Uses\nDespite its rarity, astatine is being researched for use in alpha-particle therapy for cancer. Astatine-211 can be attached to molecules that target cancer cells, providing a powerful and highly localized dose of radiation that can kill the tumor while potentially sparing surrounding healthy tissue.\n\n### Characteristics\nAstatine is a member of the halogen group. It is likely a dark, lustrous solid and may have some metallic properties. Like iodine, it is expected to be more concentrated in the thyroid gland if ingested. However, its intense radioactivity would cause massive damage long before any chemical properties became significant.\n\n### Discovery\nAstatine was first synthesized in 1940 by Dale Corson, Kenneth MacKenzie, and Emilio Segrè at the University of California, Berkeley. Its name is derived from the Greek word 'astatos,' meaning 'unstable.'",
