    period: 6, 
    summary: "Radon is a colorless, odorless, radioactive noble gas. it occurs naturally as a decay product of uranium and thorium.", 
    details: "Radon is a chemical element with the symbol Rn and atomic number 86. It is the densest known gas and is radioactive. It is chemically inert like other noble gases but is extremely dangerous due to its radioactivity.\n\n### Natural Occurrence and Hazards\nRadon is produced by the natural decay of uranium in soil and rocks. It can seep into buildings through cracks in the foundation or through water supplies. Because it is a gas, it can be inhaled, where it continues to decay and emit alpha radiation into the lungs. Radon exposure is the second leading cause of lung cancer worldwide after smoking.\n\n### Medical and Scientific Uses\nRadon was formerly used in radiotherapy for the treatment of certain cancers, though it has been largely replaced by safer and more targeted radioactive sources. It is also used in specialized geological research to track the movement of groundwater and in searching for uranium deposits.\n\n### Physical Properties\nRadon is colorless, odorless, and tasteless. When cooled below its freezing point (-71 °C), it exhibits a brilliant yellow or orange-red phosphorescence. It is monatomic and is the only radioactive gas under standard conditions.\n\n### Discovery\nRadon was discovered in 1900 by the German physicist Friedrich Ernst Dorn while he was investigating the decay products of radium. It was the fifth radioactive element discovered, after uranium, thorium, radium, and polonium.",
