    summary: "Francium is a highly radioactive alkali metal. It is the second rarest naturally occurring element after astatine.", 
    details: "Francium is a chemical element with the symbol Fr and atomic number 87. It is one of the most unstable and highly radioactive elements. It occurs naturally in trace amounts in uranium and thorium ores, with less than 30 grams existing in the Earth's crust at any one time.\n\n### Extreme Radioactivity and Rarity\nFrancium has no stable isotopes. The most stable isotope, francium-223, has a half-life of only 22 minutes. Because it decays so rapidly and is so rare, it is extremely difficult to study. Most of its properties have been inferred from its position in the alkali metal group or through specialized experiments with tiny, synthetically produced samples.\n\n### Potential Characteristics\nAs an alkali metal, francium is expected to be more reactive than caesium. It is likely a heavy, soft metal with a low melting point. However, its intense radioactivity causes it to release so much heat that a macroscopic sample would probably vaporize itself instantly.\n\n### Discovery\nFrancium was discovered in 1939 by Marguerite Perey at the Curie Institute in Paris. She identified it as a decay product of actinium-227. It was the last element to be discovered in nature before being synthetically produced. She named it after her native country, France.",
