    period: 7, 
    summary: "Radium is a silvery-white, highly radioactive alkaline earth metal. It was famously discovered by Marie and Pierre Curie.", 
    details: "Radium is a chemical element with the symbol Ra and atomic number 88. It is the sixth element in the alkaline earth metal group. All isotopes of radium are highly radioactive, and it glow with a faint blue light in the dark due to its intense radioactivity.\n\n### Historical Importance and 'Radium Girls'\nIn the early 20th century, radium was used in luminous paints for watch dials and aircraft instruments. This led to a major occupational health scandal involving the 'Radium Girls,' female factory workers who suffered horrific bone damage and deaths from radium ingestion. This case was pivotal in establishing modern occupational safety standards.\n\n### Medical and Industrial Uses\nRadium was formerly used in radiotherapy to treat cancer (brachytherapy), though it has been largely replaced by other radioactive sources like cobalt-60. Today, its primary use is as a source for producing radon gas for specialized research and as a neutron source when mixed with beryllium.\n\n### Toxicity and Handling\nRadium is extremely hazardous. When ingested, the body treats it like calcium and deposits it in the bones, where its alpha radiation destroys bone marrow and causes mutations. It must be handled with extreme care in specialized laboratories.\n\n### Discovery\nRadium was discovered in 1898 by Marie and Pierre Curie. They extracted it from pitchblende (uranium ore) and found it was much more radioactive than uranium. Its name comes from the Latin 'radius,' meaning ray, referring to its intense radioactivity.",
