    symbol: "Cf", 
    name: "Californium", 
    atomicMass: "(251)", 
    category: "actinide", 
    group: 12, 
    period: 10, 
    summary: "Californium is a highly radioactive transuranic metal. It is one of the heaviest elements produced in significant quantities.", 
    details: "Californium is a chemical element with the symbol Cf and atomic number 98. It is a member of the actinide series. It was first synthesized in 1950 by the team of Glenn T. Seaborg at the University of California, Berkeley.\n\n### Powerful Neutron Source\nCalifornium-252 is a very powerful neutron emitter. A single microgram of californium-252 can produce over 170 million neutrons per minute. This makes it incredibly useful for various applications, including neutron activation analysis to detect gold and silver ores, and in neutron moisture gauges for searching for water and oil layers in wells.\n\n### Medical and Industrial Uses\nIn medicine, californium-252 is used in specialized brachytherapy for the treatment of certain types of brain and cervical cancers. It is also used to start up nuclear reactors and as a portable neutron source for inspecting aircraft components for hidden corrosion and for scanning luggage for explosives.\n\n### Synthesis and Rarity\nCalifornium is produced in specialized high-flux nuclear reactors by bombarding curium with neutrons. It is extremely expensive and difficult to produce, with only about half a gram created globally each year. The most stable isotope, californium-251, has a half-life of 898 years.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nCalifornium was discovered by bombarding curium-242 with alpha particles. It was the sixth transuranic element to be discovered. It was named after the state of California and the University of California.",
