    period: 10, 
    summary: "Nobelium is a highly radioactive synthetic element. It is the penultimate member of the actinide series.", 
    details: "Nobelium is a chemical element with the symbol No and atomic number 102. It is a transuranic radioactive member of the actinide series. It was first successfully identified in 1966 by researchers at the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research (JINR) in Dubna, Russia.\n\n### Synthesis and 'The Transfermium Wars'\nThe discovery of nobelium was controversial. Several groups in the US, Sweden, and Russia claimed to have synthesized it between 1957 and 1966. This rivalry, known as the 'Transfermium Wars,' led to a long dispute over the naming rights of several heavy elements. The IUPAC eventually credited the Dubna team with the first definitive discovery.\n\n### Scientific Research\nNobelium has no uses outside of basic scientific research. It is primarily studied to investigate the chemical properties of the heaviest actinides and to understand the 'Relativistic Effects' on electrons in superheavy atoms. The most stable isotope, nobelium-259, has a half-life of 58 minutes.\n\n### Characteristics\nWhile nobelium metal has never been seen in macroscopic amounts, it is predicted to be a silvery radioactive metal. In solution, it is more stable in the +2 oxidation state than any other actinide, showing its unique position at the end of the series.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nNobelium was synthesized by bombarding curium targets with carbon ions. It was named after Alfred Nobel, the Swedish chemist who invented dynamite and established the Nobel Prizes.",
