    symbol: "Lr", 
    name: "Lawrencium", 
    atomicMass: "(266)", 
    category: "actinide", 
    group: 17, 
    period: 10, 
    summary: "Lawrencium is a highly radioactive synthetic element. It is the final element of the actinide series.", 
    details: "Lawrencium is a chemical element with the symbol Lr and atomic number 103. It is the last member of the actinide series and is a d-block element. It was first synthesized in 1961 by a team at the University of California, Berkeley.\n\n### Synthesis and Discovery\nLawrencium was created by bombarding a target of californium isotopes with boron ions in a heavy-ion linear accelerator (HILAC). It decays rapidly, with the most stable isotope, lawrencium-266, having a half-life of about 11 hours. Most other isotopes decay in seconds or milliseconds.\n\n### Scientific Study\nLike other superheavy elements, lawrencium has no practical applications. It is studied to understand the behavior of the outermost electrons in superheavy atoms. Scientists have found that lawrencium has a unique electron configuration that marks the end of the actinide 5f-shell filling.\n\n### Characteristics\nLawrencium is expected to be a silvery radioactive metal. It is highly unstable and difficult to produce. Because of its short half-life, all studies are conducted with only a few atoms at a time.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nThe discovery team was led by Albert Ghiorso and included Torbjørn Sikkeland, Almon Larsh, and Robert M. Latimer. It was named after Ernest Lawrence, the inventor of the cyclotron and the founder of the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory.",
