    symbol: "Db", 
    name: "Dubnium", 
    atomicMass: "(268)", 
    category: "transition metal", 
    group: 5, 
    period: 7, 
    summary: "Dubnium is a highly radioactive synthetic element. It is a member of the transactinide series.", 
    details: "Dubnium is a chemical element with the symbol Db and atomic number 105. It is a transactinide element and a d-block transition metal. It was first produced in 1968/1970.\n\n### Synthesis and Discovery Dispute\nDubnium was another element involved in the 'Transfermium Wars.' Scientists at the JINR in Dubna, Russia, and the LBNL in Berkeley, California, both claimed discovery. The Russian team used neon ions to bombard americium, while the American team used nitrogen ions to bombard californium. The IUPAC eventually shared credit between the two labs.\n\n### Short Half-Life\nAll isotopes of dubnium are extremely unstable. The most stable isotope, dubnium-268, has a half-life of 16 hours. Because it decays so rapidly, only a few atoms can be studied at a time. Experiments have shown its chemical properties are similar to tantalum and niobium.\n\n### Scientific Study\nLike other superheavy elements, dubnium is primarily used for fundamental research into nuclear physics and the chemistry of the heaviest elements. It has no known applications outside of the laboratory.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nDubnium was named after the city of Dubna in Russia, the location of the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research where it was first synthesized. This name was part of a compromise to settle the discovery disputes of several heavy elements.",
