    details: "Bohrium is a chemical element with the symbol Bh and atomic number 107. It is a transactinide element and a d-block transition metal. It was first synthesized in 1981 by a team at the GSI Helmholtz Centre for Heavy Ion Research in Darmstadt, Germany.\n\n### Discovery and 'Cold Fusion'\nThe discovery team, led by Peter Armbruster and Gottfried Münzenberg, synthesized bohrium by bombarding a bismuth-209 target with chromium-54 ions. This technique, called 'cold fusion' (not to be confused with the dismissed energy source), allowed scientists to produce superheavy elements with lower excitation energy, increasing the chances of survival for the new nuclei.\n\n### Instability and Chemistry\nAll isotopes of bohrium are extremely short-lived. The most stable known isotope, bohrium-270, has a half-life of about 61 seconds. Chemical experiments conducted on a few atoms at a time suggest that bohrium behaves like a heavier version of rhenium, forming a volatile heptoxide.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nBohrium was named after the Danish physicist Niels Bohr, who made fundamental contributions to the understanding of atomic structure and quantum mechanics. The name was part of a compromise to settle the naming controversies of the 1990s.",
