    details: "Hassium is a chemical element with the symbol Hs and atomic number 108. It is a transactinide element and a transition metal. it was first synthesized in 1984 by a team at the GSI in Darmstadt, Germany.\n\n### Synthesis and Discovery\nThe discovery team, led by Peter Armbruster and Gottfried Münzenberg, bombarded a lead-208 target with iron-58 ions. This produced isotopes of hassium that decay rapidly. The most stable isotope, hassium-269, has a half-life of only 16 seconds.\n\n### Relativistic Chemistry\nHassium is expected to be a heavier analog of osmium. Chemical experiments have successfully shown that hassium reacts with oxygen to form hassium tetroxide (HsO4), which is volatile and has similar properties to osmium tetroxide. This was a major achievement, demonstrating that the chemical trends of the periodic table continue even into the superheavy element region, despite strong 'relativistic effects' on the atom's electrons.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nHassium was named after 'Hassia,' the Latin name for the German state of Hesse (Hessen), where the GSI laboratory is located.",
