    details: "Meitnerium is a chemical element with the symbol Mt and atomic number 109. It is a transactinide element and is likely a transition metal. It was first synthesized in 1982 by a team at the GSI in Darmstadt, Germany.\n\n### Synthesis and Rarity\nMeitnerium was produced by bombarding a bismuth-209 target with iron-58 ions. Only a single atom was detected in the first successful experiment. Since then, very few atoms have been produced, primarily in studies of its decay properties. The most stable known isotope, meitnerium-278, has a half-life of only 4.5 seconds.\n\n### Predicted Properties\nDue to the extremely short half-lives of its isotopes and the low production rates, no chemical experiments have yet been performed on meitnerium. Based on its position in the periodic table, it is expected to be a noble metal and a heavier homolog of iridium. It is likely a dense, silvery solid at room temperature.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nMeitnerium was named in honor of Lise Meitner, the Austrian-Swedish physicist who co-discovered nuclear fission and made many other fundamental contributions to nuclear physics. It was the only element named exclusively after a non-mythological woman until the naming of curium (which honors both Marie and Pierre Curie).",
