    details: "Darmstadtium is a chemical element with the symbol Ds and atomic number 110. It is a transactinide element and is likely a d-block transition metal. It was first synthesized in 1994 at the GSI in Darmstadt, Germany.\n\n### Synthesis and Discovery\nThe discovery team, led by Sigurd Hofmann, bombarded a lead-208 target with nickel-62 ions to create darmstadtium-269. Like other superheavy elements, it decays very rapidly. The most stable known isotope, darmstadtium-281, has a half-life of about 14 seconds.\n\n### Predicted Properties\nNo chemical experiments have been performed on darmstadtium due to its instability. Based on its position in group 10, it is expected to be a noble metal and a heavier analog of platinum. It is predicted to be a very dense, silvery-white metal that is resistant to corrosion.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nIt was the fourth element discovered at the GSI and was named after the city of Darmstadt to honor its status as a major center for heavy ion research. The name was officially adopted by the IUPAC in 2003.",
