    details: "Nihonium is a chemical element with the symbol Nh and atomic number 113. It is a transactinide element and is likely a post-transition metal. It was first successfully identified in 2004 by researchers at the RIKEN Nishina Center for Accelerator-Based Science in Japan.\n\n### Discovery in Japan\nNihonium was the first element to be discovered and named by a team from an Asian country. The RIKEN team, led by Kosuke Morita, bombarded a bismuth-209 target with zinc-70 ions. Their success was a major milestone for Japanese science. Other teams in the US and Russia also claimed discovery around the same time.\n\n### Extreme Instability\nAll isotopes of nihonium are extremely unstable and short-lived. The most stable known isotope, nihonium-286, has a half-life of only 9.5 seconds. This makes chemical studies almost impossible. It is expected to be a heavy, silvery metal that is more volatile than its neighbor thallium.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nThe name 'nihonium' is derived from 'Nihon,' one of the two Japanese words for Japan. The name was officially adopted by the IUPAC in 2016, along with those of elements 115, 117, and 118.",
