    details: "Tennessine is a chemical element with the symbol Ts and atomic number 117. It is a transactinide element and a member of the halogen group. It was first synthesized in 2010 by a joint team of Russian and American scientists at the JINR in Dubna.\n\n### Last Naturally Occurring Group Completion\nThe synthesis of tennessine was a major milestone because it completed group 17, the halogens. It was produced by bombarding a berkelium-249 target with calcium-48 ions. The target was produced at the Oak Ridge National Laboratory in Tennessee, which was critical to the project's success.\n\n### Instability and Predictions\nAll isotopes of tennessine are highly unstable. The most stable known isotope, tennessine-294, has a half-life of only about 51 milliseconds. Based on its position, it is expected to be a volatile solid and might even show some metallic properties, unlike the lighter halogens.\n\n### Discovery and Naming\nTennessine was named after the US state of Tennessee, in recognition of the contributions of researchers from that state (Oak Ridge, Vanderbilt University, and the University of Tennessee) to the discovery of the element. The name was officially adopted in 2016.",
